{"title":"2007 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2007 AMC 12A Problems\">2007 AMC 12A #11</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_22\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2007 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 22\">2007 AMC 10A #22</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Principle_of_Fractions)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Principle of Fractions)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Post_Problem_Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Post Problem Note</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Using_the_provided_sequence/Guessing)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Using the provided sequence/Guessing)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(a_better_way_of_doing_solution_3)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (a better way of doing solution 3)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A finite <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sequence\" title=\"Sequence\">sequence</a> of three-digit integers has the property that the tens and units digits of each term are, respectively, the hundreds and tens digits of the next term, and the tens and units digits of the last term are, respectively, the hundreds and tens digits of the first term. For example, such a sequence might begin with the terms 247, 475, and 756 and end with the term 824. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the sum of all the terms in the sequence. What is the largest <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime\">prime</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Factor\" title=\"Factor\">factor</a> that always divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />? \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/2/3e22f2ebeb1adfccf80fd9b3650c640aa3c05116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A)}\\ 3\\qquad \\mathrm{(B)}\\ 7\\qquad \\mathrm{(C)}\\ 13\\qquad \\mathrm{(D)}\\ 37\\qquad \\mathrm{(E)}\\ 43$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"372\" height=\"20\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A given digit appears as the hundreds digit, the tens digit, and the units digit of a term the same number of times. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> be the sum of the units digits in all the terms. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/e/a8e78ec8ff4907fdc748607088b1ce2b76af1c57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=111k=3 \\cdot 37k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"150\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/f/7ef5b09e5c4614dd7bdb5bfcbe3997e13ead3420.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$37\\ \\mathrm{(D)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. To see that it need not be divisible by any larger prime, the sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/2/292c0a8c3f05f6f0b13602393fdaa4f2cde9a997.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$123, 231, 312$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"97\" height=\"16\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/f/b2fd832a94f66ec386e9c1688853e3e0192b40d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=666=2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 37\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{\\boxed{(D)~~37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"258\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Principle_of_Fractions.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Principle_of_Fractions)\">Solution 2 (Principle of Fractions)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Principle of Fractions)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the first term be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c5a1ceb95b62c9c34572681c80bee6671a5c9d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100a+10b+c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"115\" height=\"14\" />. The next term, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/1/8b19ae6b600fb40aa0e15f1bdf40b1c3485e1084.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100b+10c+d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"117\" height=\"16\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/b/1bbf4c343650f38d11da8e1fcfda25f3eaaf00d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100c+10d+e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"118\" height=\"16\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/6/7f69c3e68569f5d17c000002f100303e888cc52e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100d+10e+f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />, and so on until <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/e/f1eecf2d67268752d36b74916e16136af61f4dd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100w+10x+y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"17\" /> and then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/f/60f77ed56a012da687f53d23bb3003ff99c21121.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100x+10y+z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"118\" height=\"16\" />. Then, the sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is just the first term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/4/7c49bbb04efc0698fce6fc26d362198cf94ee8e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"39\" height=\"14\" />, added with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/1/611e970696ff3759f1b7d77c4260105955148ede.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$110b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"14\" />, and then added with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/7/7377c9a2f3cf09ed75db1b7e2bde68fb9b2b44ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$111(c+d+\\dots+x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"159\" height=\"20\" />, followed by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/1/801ea165833d9657d9d1237b5bcdd9ede2c0f9b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" /> and finally <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. In mathematical terms,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/b/e7b4f8619a5509c0a356e01b8e214a355ce59aa6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S = 100a+110b+111(c+d+\\dots+x) + 11y + z\\]\" width=\"390\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>The largest prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbc672f2deecad71150a4bd96a492515445dcf26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p~|~S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"20\" /> satisfies the property that it must equivalate itself as an integer when divided and added to all other terms or must divide one of the coefficients. If the former is true, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/6/45660cc710d97607b7a7f1b00bee99d8581bff51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100+110+111+11+1 = 333$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"251\" height=\"15\" /> must equivalate to zero modulo that prime, implying that 3 and 37 are possible equivalencies. To verify no other cases, we check the latter case. Out of all plausible prime divisors of each respective coefficient we see that the extremum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/d/58d1eed7be2e1dcbfd4f7fe86fba2eb7566d8bbf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$111$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> contains the largest, which is 37. We check modulo 37 on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> but quickly arise the same conclusion as the prior case, supporting that 37 is indeed the largest prime.\n</p><p>The requested largest prime is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/d/8dd1e60a1fcfe4a72c3516a799d3a39acbcb20b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{37}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"31\" height=\"25\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Post_Problem_Note\">Post Problem Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Post Problem Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Note that here, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are interchangeable for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> given the problem directory, and by substitution one will arrive to the same conclusion as solution 1.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Using_the_provided_sequence.2FGuessing.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Using_the_provided_sequence/Guessing)\">Solution 3 (Using the provided sequence/Guessing)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Using the provided sequence/Guessing)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since a prime number will always divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, the prime number must work on any sequence that follows the stated rule. Since the solution kindly provides us with the terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/6/ab6cc342fb920f6d308c4bc86ad14414c0948f2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$247,475,756,824$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"17\" />,all we have to do is finish the sequence.\n</p><p>One way of finishing the sequence is by adding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/4/1a450ebad12196e88e3b21ff853cb1f4502245a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$568,682$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"17\" /> (before 824) to the sequence and adding them all together. After this, we just divide the result by each of the answer choices. Of the five, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/78916550838bd31d66e4cdefbc366edb11fd00bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$37$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" /> is the largest prime number that divides this sequence, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/5/f3560be0e6651731c1e5df8b6a3f4ccbb3dad230.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\boxed{(D)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"43\" height=\"32\" />.\n~Banspeedrun\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28a_better_way_of_doing_solution_3.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(a_better_way_of_doing_solution_3)\">Solution 4 (a better way of doing solution 3)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (a better way of doing solution 3)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because the question says which prime number will <b> <i> ALWAYS </i></b> divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, the prime number would divide any sequence that follows the rule. So, to test this, create two sequences with three terms each- <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/6/8661bef8e4fafd507b422a7bb4d9c51b277a6c4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$111, 111, 111$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"100\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/4/fb4130ca225592d29d74fa162696e03c310641d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$222, 222, 222$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"100\" height=\"17\" />. The sum for both (respectively) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/7/467f742fba8bdced4d5ba877428a03fdfd4cb5be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$333$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/c/92ccb7728d3898f9858435673beff982bcb3ac71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$666$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />. Obviously, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> divides both. Now, test the other choices on both numbers, but don't if it doesn't divide one. An example is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/4/304065eeab568d1a623a96c348e8b4d0f64ca7b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{333}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" />. Because it doesn't divide, don't check <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/0/7509193c6745ba9a62650530c157e2c5f02d437e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{666}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" />. Use the same logic for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/a/5cab3f32fa979b9997d1f410a4a59a4b71b936d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{333}{13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" />. However, when you test <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/f/73ffc41c52e5ed691a1b738bc7b7ac792b76589a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{333}{37}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" />, it works. Now, you must check <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/8/0c8b0e7ae29eade6c442594f165c03b0b8ad0f56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{666}{37}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" /> to ensure it isn't a special case. After doing this, you get that both are divisible. Now, check <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/f/adfb8ead59db14dce7c86b4cb374a066c0d9407b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{333}{43}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" /> and see that it doesn't work. \n</p><p><br />\nSo the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/5/f3560be0e6651731c1e5df8b6a3f4ccbb3dad230.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\boxed{(D)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"43\" height=\"32\" />\n</p><p>-N.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P-phdgVAQqI\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P-phdgVAQqI</a>    ~David\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A\" title=\"2007 AMC 12A\">2007 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2007 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2007 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2007 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 10\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2007 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2007 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?tit